<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip, Duke of Parma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1720">1720</a>–<a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon-parma">House of Bourbon-Parma</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip, Duke of Parma, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a> who founded the Bourbon-Parma line. Under the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1748 he received the duchies of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla. With his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-du-tillot">Guillaume du Tillot</a> he carried out reforms that made the small court a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-duke-of-parma.jpg" alt="Philip, Duke of Parma" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Carlo Rusca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felipe_de_Borb%C3%B3n,_duque_de_Parma_(Rusca)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born at the Royal Alcazar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> as Felipe de Borbón y Farnesio, the fourth child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> and his second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>. As a child he showed more interest in art than in politics. Like other children of the Spanish royal family he was raised in the Queen's household until the age of seven, with the Marchioness of Montehermoso as his governess. In 1724, when his father abdicated in favour of his son Louis, the four-year-old infante was given the Marquis of Surco as governor of his household. He also held the title of 12th Count of Chinchón, which he later ceded to his brother Luis in 1754.</p><p>His mother came from the Farnese family, which had ruled the Duchy of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla for many generations. His elder brother Charles ruled it from 1731 to 1736, until it was exchanged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> for the Two Sicilies after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Twelve years later, in the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lost the duchy and Philip became Duke of Parma on 18 October 1748, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon-parma">House of Bourbon-Parma</a>, a cadet line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a>. Under the Second Treaty of Versailles of 1757 between Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he was meant to become king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a>, in a deal that would have let French troops occupy key positions there. The Third Treaty of Versailles repudiated the arrangement, and Philip stayed in Parma.</p><p>The duchy had been ruined by years of warfare, and in 1759 Philip named the Frenchman Guillaume du Tillot as his minister to restore the economy. Philip was an enlightened ruler who stimulated education and philosophy and attracted figures such as Étienne Bonnot de Condillac and Alexandre Deleyre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 October 1739 Philip had married his first cousin once removed, Princess Louise Élisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at Alcalá de Henares. The marriage was unhappy, and she died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> at the age of 32 in 1759. They had three children: Isabella, who married the Archduke Joseph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>; Ferdinand, who succeeded his father as duke; and Luisa Maria, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>Philip himself died unexpectedly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 18 July 1765 at Alessandria, after accompanying his daughter on her way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, where she sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to marry. Through her he is an ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> of Spain, the Bourbons of the Two Sicilies and the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip, Duke of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After he became king of Naples in 1735, Parma passed to the Habsburgs, and the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1748 then gave Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla to <b>Philip of Bourbon</b>, founding the Bourbon-Parma line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1748 the duchy was given to <b>Philip of Bourbon</b> and joined to the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guastalla">Duchy of Guastalla</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Alessandria, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1749" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philip, Duke of Parma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1749">What was here in 1749? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
